      "id": 1,
      "text": "Tracy City earned its name from financier Samuel Franklin Tracy, who helped build the town after coal was discovered in 1840.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        4,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tracy_City%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s historic downtown grew during TC&R’s boom between 1875 and 1895, shaping its streets and architecture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mountainsofadventure.com/communities/tracy-city",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Tennessee’s oldest family‑owned bakery, Dutch Maid Bakery, opened in 1902 and still serves locals in Tracy City.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mountainsofadventure.com/communities/tracy-city",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s historic coke ovens, once fueling railroads, were listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1987.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
